P1: in get_definition/go.rs ~4608, the walk's default arm ignores labeled_statement even when it wraps a short variable declaration that introduces a local with the same name. The static proof at ~73 trusts that walk. This is valid Go, checked with go run and go vet:
```go
func work(value []int, replacement error) {
    { again: value := replacement; if value != nil {}; if false { goto again } }
}
```
The inner error binding is skipped, the outer []int parameter supplies Syntax([]int), and the empty handler is suppressed again.

Fix:
(a) Unwrap labeled_statement structurally, through its statement field, in the same scope. The wrapped declaration keeps its normal visibility and redeclaration rules. Labels may nest (`a: b: x := ...`), so unwrap iteratively.
(b) Change the default arm so it stops at unknown, instead of skipping, for any statement kind that can directly introduce a binding in the enclosing scope and that the walk does not model. Use the Go grammar's node types, not a hand-picked list. Statements that open their own scope (if, for, switch, select, block, func literal) are already handled by the scope walk; say so in your audit. Report the final classification of every statement kind in the Go grammar's statement supertype: modeled, own-scope, cannot declare, or stops-unknown.
(c) Add a golden regression with the labeled form, including a nested-label variant, in the failure-handler goldens. Add a navigation probe if navigation reads it.

Identity: if (a) and (b) move no reader answers beyond the labeled shapes, v53/v52 and -7470-go-visible-binding-shapes stand. List any golden line that moves outside the new cases.

Validate as in your last run:
- 282 libs and 150 root tests;
- the reason-inventory and admission tests and the token test;
- Clippy on 8 libs, fmt and drift.
Rerun the mux oracle on the final head. Commit with the trailer, push to 7343-go-failure-handler-typed-nil with an explicit refspec, verify the head with ls-remote and gh api pulls/7470, and do not mark it ready. Report the head, the statement-kind audit and the golden lines.

Parent decision: include the for_clause fix in #7470. It is the same shared walk and the same silent-miss class, and #7470 has not landed. Its identity (v57/v56 plus -7470-go-visible-binding-shapes) covers moved reader answers, so this needs no new token or pair. Commit it separately from 2b0663d974.

Scope: every statement header that declares a binding scoped to its own statement. Audit and fix them together:
- the for_clause initializer: `for value := replacement; value != nil; { ... }`;
- the if-statement initializer: `if value := replacement; value != nil {}`;
- the expression_switch initializer: `switch value := replacement; { case value != nil: }`;
- the type_switch initializer (the statement before the alias): `switch x := f(); v := x.(type)`;
- the select communication case: `case value := <-ch:` (receive binders are opaque values, as you modeled; check that the case scope is entered);
- range, already handled: confirm it.
Each binding is visible in the header's condition and post, and in the body, and not after the statement. Use the existing structural fields (initializer, condition, and so on) or the grammar subtype metadata, as you did for labels; no source matching. For each form that fits the empty-handler shape, add a golden case with an outer binding of the same name and a different type. Report every golden line that moves outside the new cases, with its cause.

Then, when I send the pushed #7507 head: merge it, validate once on the merged head, rerun the mux oracle, push, and verify. That head will come after a validation child finishes on .mj/lanes/7471-go-shortvar; do not build there.

